WEBVTT

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:04.358
Meet MXI RoboICL.

00:00:04.638 --> 00:00:08.137
Our team builds on learning through context, with geometry

00:00:08.137 --> 00:00:10.770
that helps robots act more precisely.

00:00:12.098 --> 00:00:13.917
Start with one demonstration.

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:17.810
Short windows, sampled across time, show the model how the

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:19.218
task unfolds.

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:23.230
Each window connects what the robot sees, the actions taken,

00:00:23.522 --> 00:00:24.718
and what happens next.

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:27.680
The current episode follows the same format.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.572
Selected earlier interactions and the latest result join the

00:00:31.572 --> 00:00:33.817
demonstration in the model’s context.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:37.292
The model decides, acts, and observes again.

00:00:37.585 --> 00:00:38.868
Its weights stay frozen.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.442
Experience changes the conversation.

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:50.615
Here is the same purple pen in the expert demonstration and the
current episode.

00:00:50.615 --> 00:00:54.940
MXI projects the grasp center into both wrist views.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:57.077
Now watch the left hand approach.

00:00:57.077 --> 00:00:59.302
The pen moves toward the cross.

00:00:59.302 --> 00:01:02.902
The fingers close, and the pen rises with the hand.

00:01:02.902 --> 00:01:08.502
A shared geometric reference helps the policy relate its own grasp to
the demonstration.

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:12.798
A pixel tells us a direction, but not a distance.

00:01:13.078 --> 00:01:17.348
Now match the same vase opening in two calibrated camera views.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.685
Each observation defines a ray.

00:01:19.952 --> 00:01:23.840
Together, they constrain a three dimensional position, without

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:24.735
a depth map.

00:01:25.065 --> 00:01:28.410
Here, the reconstructed depth is about half a metre.

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:31.722
The third view is occluded, so it is excluded.

00:01:32.015 --> 00:01:35.998
Back in the recorded rollout, the robot uses geometric information

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:37.348
while adjusting the pour.

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:41.572
A target is only the beginning.

00:01:41.852 --> 00:01:43.972
The arm still needs a route it can execute.

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:47.785
Here, the first preview fails inverse kinematics.

00:01:48.078 --> 00:01:49.260
No motion is sent.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.085
A revised plan passes.

00:01:51.365 --> 00:01:54.460
Now watch a translucent preview of the recorded arm motion:

00:01:54.765 --> 00:01:58.648
approach, grasp, lift, transfer, and pour.

00:01:58.928 --> 00:02:01.098
Joint targets change along the route.

00:02:01.378 --> 00:02:05.290
This preview explains the accepted sequence; it is not a second

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:06.435
physical robot.

00:02:06.715 --> 00:02:10.772
The overlay clears, and the recorded arm completes the task.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:15.038
On 22 tasks shared with the original report,

00:02:15.038 --> 00:02:19.830
the category-balanced score rose from 52.27 to 80.58.

00:02:43.938 --> 00:02:47.058
MXI RoboICL.

00:02:47.351 --> 00:02:49.233
An MXI team upgrade.

00:02:49.538 --> 00:02:50.421
Thank you for watching.
